-- title:
--   Existence of a Fricke automorphism at prime level
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a prime. The theorem asserts the existence of an automorphism $\sigma$ of the field $F_\ell =$ `modularFunctionField ℓ` as a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra, where $F_\ell$ is the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the two elements $\mathtt{jq}$ and $\mathtt{qExpand}\,\mathbb{Q}\,\ell\,\mathtt{jq}$; here `qExpand ℚ ℓ` is the ring homomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ obtained by multiplying all exponents by $\ell$, that is, the substitution $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$, realised as the `embDomainRingHom` along multiplication by $\ell$ on the exponent group $\mathbb{Z}$. The automorphism produced satisfies the predicate `IsFrickeAut ℓ`, which says exactly that $\sigma$ interchanges the two distinguished elements of $F_\ell$: the element $\mathtt{jq}$ (with its membership witness `jq_mem ℓ`) is sent to the element $\mathtt{jqN}\ \ell$ (with witness `jqN_mem ℓ`), and conversely $\mathtt{jqN}\ \ell$ is sent to $\mathtt{jq}$. Only these two equalities on generators are asserted; the statement does not in itself record that $\sigma$ has order two as a map on $F_\ell$.
--
--   This is the existence of the Fricke involution on the function field of $X_0(\ell)$ for prime level, in its concrete incarnation as the $q$-expansion field $\mathbb{Q}(j(q), j(q^{\ell}))$, where it exchanges the two generators. It feeds the construction of the Fricke automorphism used in the Atkin–Lehner theory of the project, and is cited by [`ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAutFull`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAutFull).
